# ── The two Django project packages, whole ──────────────────────────────────
# Mirrors the gate's `promop/**` / `ctomop/**` in .github/scripts/security-review.cjs.
# An owner entry covering less than the gate asks for no reviewer on a change
# the gate will still hold, which reads as the gate being broken rather than as
# the owner list being stale — so the two lists have the same shape.
#
# Twenty files, 1228 lines. settings.py (auth backends, SECRET_KEY, CORS),
# oauth.py (which clients may still authenticate), sentry.py (what leaves the
# process) and urls.py (the only permission_classes=[AllowAny] in the tree,
# plus the admin and OAuth2 mounts) are the obvious ones. wsgi.py, asgi.py and
# celery.py are not obvious and matter as much: each carries the
# `os.environ.setdefault('DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE', ...)` that picks which
# settings module production loads. Naming the obvious four would drop the
# owner from the other twelve, and from the twenty-first file when it lands,
# and would save nothing: over the last 12 months no commit needed this owner
# for the directory form that did not already need it for the named one.
/promop/                            @larsburgess
/ctomop/                            @larsburgess
# The third copy of that setdefault, and the only one outside both packages:
# start.sh runs `manage.py check --deploy` with no DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE set.
/manage.py                          @larsburgess

